    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f49671c0-4b12-5a03-bb8e-4b5c752fccfa",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + x - 2 y^{2} - 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,1)$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=1 - 2 x \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- 4 y \\\\ \\nabla f(1,1)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-4\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(1,1)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(-1\\right)^2+\\left(-4\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(1,1)}{\\|\\nabla f(1,1)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-4\\right)\\,j}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{17}}{17}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.",
